(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-nodules-neoplasms-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which change in a nodule over time is concerning?","choices":["Calcifying centrally","Enlarging in size","Staying the same size","Shrinking in size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A nodule that is enlarging in size over time is a concerning feature, together with size over 6 mm, upper lobe or hilar location, a spiculated border, more than three nodules, contrast enhancement and a cavitated or nonsolid appearance. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A lung cancer of any size has spread to one site outside the chest, the adrenal gland; which stage is it?","choices":["Stage 3B","Stage 3C","Stage 4A","Stage 4B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage 4A covers spread to the other lung, into the lining of the lung or heart with secondary nodules, into the fluid around the lungs or heart, or to one site outside the chest, such as the adrenal gland. Spread to multiple sites outside the chest would make it stage 4B. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 28, 29"},{"q":"A smoothly marginated solid nodule with central calcification on computed tomography is typical of what?","choices":["Hamartoma","Invasive adenocarcinoma","Linear scar","Healed granuloma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A smoothly marginated solid nodule with central calcification is typical of a healed granuloma, an inflammatory lesion and one of the two main benign nodules. Central calcification is also one of the benign calcification patterns, with diffuse, popcorn and concentric. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16, 21, 32"},{"q":"In the diagnostic workup of a lung nodule or lung cancer symptoms, which imaging is standard before biopsy?","choices":["Low-dose computed tomography","Magnetic resonance imaging","Contrast computed tomography","Chest radiograph"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The standard is computed tomography with contrast of the chest and upper abdomen, followed by biopsy or interventional treatment; positron emission tomography and genetic testing are also part of the standard workup. A chest radiograph is not a good tool here. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 14, 18, 43"},{"q":"In lung cancer, fatigue, weight loss, dizziness, hypotension and a high potassium point to metastasis where?","choices":["Adrenal glands","The brain","The liver","The spine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metastasis that causes adrenal insufficiency produces fatigue, weight loss, dizziness, hypotension and electrolyte imbalance, hyponatremia with hyperkalemia, often with no respiratory symptoms at all. The high potassium with low blood pressure is the clue that separates it from the other metastatic sites. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A nodular opacity lying next to the minor fissure on computed tomography is typical of what?","choices":["Scar or lymphoid tissue","Invasive adenocarcinoma","Satellite tumor nodule","Healed granuloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A nodular opacity adjacent to the minor fissure is typical of a linear scar or lymphoid tissue, and a nodule close to a fissure usually indicates something benign. It is one of the benign computed tomography findings, with the hamartoma and the healed granuloma. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21, 22, 32"},{"q":"A lung cancer has spread to both the adrenal gland and the bones; which stage is it?","choices":["Stage 3B","Stage 3C","Stage 4A","Stage 4B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage 4B is a tumor of any size, with or without nodal spread, that has spread to multiple sites outside the chest, such as the adrenal gland and the bones. Spread to only one site outside the chest would be stage 4A. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 28, 29"},{"q":"Neuroendocrine paraneoplastic symptoms arise when a lung carcinoma develops from cells that secrete polypeptide hormones; which type does this?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Hamartoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma can develop neuroendocrine paraneoplastic symptoms when it arises from cells that secrete polypeptide hormones, the neuroendocrine cells lining the bronchioles. Paraneoplastic syndromes are typically a consequence of the small cell type, such as excess antidiuretic hormone with hyponatremia. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 6, 12, 16, 31, 32, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"What is the first evaluation of a lung nodule found incidentally on imaging done for another reason?","choices":["Check sputum cytology","Repeat chest radiograph","Estimate its size","Biopsy the nodule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Incidental nodules turn up on a chest radiograph or chest computed tomography done for another reason, and the first evaluation is to estimate size. Anything smaller than 6 mm is generally not concerning unless the patient is at high risk, while a nodule over 6 mm draws attention. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 14, 16"},{"q":"Besides regional lymph nodes, to which organs does lung cancer typically spread?","choices":["Spleen, skin and thyroid","Breast and ovary","Liver, brain and bone","Small intestine and bowel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer spreads to regional lymph nodes and then to distant organs, the liver, brain and bone, as it produces new blood vessels to exceed its boundaries and its cells become poorly differentiated. Over 70% of small cell carcinomas have already reached the liver, bone or brain by detection. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 33, 41"},{"q":"Why is small cell carcinoma usually metastatic before it is detected?","choices":["Its tumors stay tiny","It skips the lymph nodes","It develops insidiously","It arises peripherally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is central and infiltrates the mucosa, which makes its development insidious, so over 70% of patients are metastatic, to the liver, bone or brain, before detection. Once the tumor is large enough for symptoms it also has a high cavitation rate. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 41, 42"},{"q":"Biopsy confirms stage 1 non-small cell lung cancer in a patient who is not fit for surgery; which treatment follows?","choices":["Lobectomy","Anatomical segmentectomy","Wedge resection","Radiotherapy or ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Resection requires a patient who can tolerate surgery. When a patient is unfit, image-guided biopsy is done if possible and safe, and confirmed malignancy is treated with stereotactic ablative radiotherapy, radiofrequency ablation or conventional radical radiotherapy; if biopsy is unsafe, those treatments proceed anyway. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 35"},{"q":"Why do chemotherapy and radiation serve mainly as adjuncts in non-small cell lung cancer?","choices":["It is usually caught early","It responds poorly to them","Radiation scars the lung","Targeted drugs replaced them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chemotherapy and radiation are an adjunct to surgery, or used in stage 4, because non-small cell lung cancers do not respond well to them. Small cell carcinoma is the contrast: it responds better to chemotherapy and radiation than the three non-small cell types. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 31, 35, 41, 45"},{"q":"Excess antidiuretic hormone from small cell lung cancer produces which sodium and osmolality pattern?","choices":["High sodium, high osmolality","Low sodium, high osmolality","High sodium, low osmolality","Low sodium, low osmolality"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Excess antidiuretic hormone, vasopressin, makes the body retain too much water, so hyponatremia and decreased serum osmolality result. These cause confusion, seizures, ataxia and other neurologic dysfunction, without peripheral or pulmonary edema. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 48"},{"q":"What is the main downside of annual lung cancer screening?","choices":["Missed early cancers","High radiation dose","Delayed treatment","False positives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening has a high incidence of false positives that prove benign, so patients receive an unnecessary workup that could have complications, such as those of a biopsy. The upside is early detection, which allows rapid intervention and an improved prognosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In which stage of small cell carcinoma does radiation work best?","choices":["Any stage equally","Limited stage","Extensive stage","Combined type"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma is divided by metastasis into limited stage, confined to one area with only local lymph nodes involved, extensive stage, spread away from the local area, and combined. Radiation works best when the cancer is in limited stage. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 41, 43"},{"q":"What do carcinogens do to oncogenes and tumor suppressor genes in lung cells?","choices":["Activate both gene groups","Inactivate both gene groups","Activate oncogenes, inactivate suppressors","Inactivate oncogenes, activate suppressors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carcinogens, or other causes of genetic mutation, activate oncogenes in lung cells, and further mutations or loss of regulatory control inactivate the tumor suppressor genes. Together these let atypical cells proliferate and grow, starting locally and then spreading. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are solitary nodules seen on a chest radiograph sometimes called?","choices":["Ground-glass nodules","Coin lesions","Satellite nodules","Popcorn lesions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A solitary nodule seen on a chest radiograph is sometimes called a coin lesion. Most such nodules are benign, mainly granulomas or hamartomas, but a patient at high risk for lung cancer needs screening and workup to exclude malignancy. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16, 20, 22"},{"q":"Local invasion by squamous cell carcinoma toward the laryngeal nerves causes which later sign?","choices":["Voice changes","Papilledema","Leg weakness","Cutaneous flushing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma metastasizes by local invasion, producing later signs such as dysphagia and voice changes when it spreads to the laryngeal nerves. Its earlier symptoms, cough, wheezing and hemoptysis, follow obstruction of the bronchus. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 39, 47, 48"},{"q":"A central lung cancer that cavitates, obstructs a bronchus and raises the serum calcium is most likely which type?","choices":["Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma is central, often arising in the bronchus, with frequent cavitation and obstruction, and hypercalcemia is the paraneoplastic syndrome linked particularly to it. The raised calcium is the clue that separates it from the other central type, small cell carcinoma. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 33, 39, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"For a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm with a low-to-moderate clinical probability of malignancy, what is the next step?","choices":["Serial computed tomography","Thoracoscopic resection","Sputum cytology","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A low-to-moderate probability nodule is assessed with fludeoxyglucose positron emission tomography. Negative or mild uptake sends it to surveillance scans; moderate or intense uptake leads to nonsurgical biopsy or video-assisted thoracoscopic resection, with subspecialist referral. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 14, 17"},{"q":"Which nodule appearance suggests that it is benign?","choices":["Lobulated borders","Cavitated center","Well-defined borders","Spiculated borders"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Benign nodules tend to have well-defined borders, and they may contain fatty tissue or popcorn-looking calcification, both pointing to a hamartoma. On computed tomography the benign examples are smoothly marginated solid nodules. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"About what share of lung cancers occur in people under 40 years old?","choices":["About 4%","About 15%","About 25%","About 40%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most lung cancers occur in people over 50, but about 4% occur in people under 40. The share is small but real, so a young age alone does not exclude lung cancer in a patient with persistent cough, hemoptysis or weight loss. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 12"},{"q":"Which step comes directly before an invasive malignant tumor in lung cancer growth?","choices":["Metastasis","In situ cancer","Hyperplasia","Dysplasia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Growth runs from a genetically altered epithelial cell to hyperplasia, where cells divide faster than normal, then dysplasia, where cells change form, then in situ cancer, where cells stay in one place. Next comes the malignant tumor that invades normal tissue and enters blood and lymph. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which central lung cancer can be diagnosed with sputum cytology?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma arises in the central bronchi, and unlike the other lung cancers it can be caught with sputum testing, even though sputum cytology is otherwise not sensitive. Its bronchial origin is also why cough, wheezing and hemoptysis appear once it obstructs. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 33, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"Which peripheral lung cancer metastasizes to unusual sites such as the small intestine and bowel?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma forms in the periphery and can metastasize to rare areas such as the small intestine and bowel, a feature that sets it apart. It is anaplastic and undifferentiated, forms necrosis and hemorrhage readily and shows a large cavitated mass on computed tomography. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 31, 33, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"In tumor, node and metastasis staging of lung cancer, what does N1 describe?","choices":["Same-side hilar nodes","Same-side mediastinal nodes","Opposite-side hilar nodes","Supraclavicular nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. N1 is spread to ipsilateral, same-side, peribronchial or hilar nodes and intrapulmonary nodes. N2 is same-side mediastinal or subcarinal nodes, and N3 is contralateral mediastinal or hilar nodes or any scalene or supraclavicular node. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"For a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm with a very low clinical probability of malignancy, what comes next?","choices":["Nonsurgical biopsy","Thoracoscopic resection","Serial computed tomography","Positron emission tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the clinical probability of malignancy is very low, the nodule is followed with computed tomography surveillance at 3 to 6, 9 to 12 and 18 to 24 months, using low-dose, thin-section, noncontrast scans. Positron emission tomography is reserved for the low-to-moderate group. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is lepidic growth in adenocarcinoma?","choices":["Spread along the alveolar lining","Invasion of the stroma","Invasion of the pleura","Spread to hilar lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After starting as atypical adenomatous hyperplasia and growing in situ, adenocarcinoma either enlarges by lepidic growth across the alveolar lining or begins to invade the stroma. Stromal invasion defines minimally invasive adenocarcinoma, and pleural and vascular invasion define invasive disease. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 32, 40"},{"q":"Which lung cancer type is also called oat cell carcinoma?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell lung carcinoma is also called oat cell carcinoma and makes up 15 to 20% of lung cancers. It is the type that typically produces paraneoplastic syndromes, sits centrally and is usually metastatic by the time of diagnosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 30, 31, 41, 42"}]};
